> # llama.cpp fork: 1.7x to 2.4x faster decode on MoE models bigger than your VRAM

The newest mixture-of-experts models (GLM-5.3-Flash, MiMo-V2.6-Flash, Qwen3.8-Flash-Next, Qwen3.6) are far bigger than a gaming GPU.
This fork keeps their experts in RAM and turns the free VRAM into a live cache of the experts the model is using; the GPUs compute
the cached ones and the CPU the rest, at the same time. No special switches needed.

```sh
llama-server -m GLM-5.3-Flash-GSQ-RCO-3.0bit-q4kattn.gguf
llama ... (truncated)
Thank you for sharing the LLAMA fork's performance comparison with the stock llama.cpp on different machines and models. Here's a summary of the key findings:

1. The fork shows significant performance improvements when a third to a half of a MoE model fits in VRAM. The largest gain is observed when about 55% of the model fits in VRAM, with a 1.9x to 2.4x speedup.
2. The gain over stock llama.cpp is the share of the model that fits in VRAM. For example, the GLM 3.5-bit model on A gains 2.2x, while the Qwen Next UD-IQ4_XS 88 GB on A gains 1.7x.
3. The decode speedup is largest when a third to a half of the model fits in VRAM. The gain decreases as the model size approaches 100%.
4. The performance of the fork is comparable to stock

